(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-01"] = {"chem-12-01-m01":{"idea":"Solids are either crystalline or amorphous. The difference is in how far the order of particles goes.","steps":["Glass and rubber are amorphous solids.","Their particles have only short-range order, not a long regular lattice.","Because the order is poor, they soften slowly over a range of temperature.","This matches the choice about short-range order."],"others":"Equal properties in all directions from a crystal lattice is wrong, because amorphous solids have no lattice. A sharp melting point belongs to crystalline solids. A regular pattern over long distances is the crystalline description.","trap":"Sharp melting point means crystalline; softening over a range means amorphous.","answer":"its particles have only short-range order, and it softens over a range of temperature"},"chem-12-01-m02":{"idea":"Anisotropic means direction dependent. Crystals show this.","steps":["In a crystal the particles are arranged differently along different directions.","So some properties, such as refractive index or electrical conductivity, change with direction.","This is what anisotropy means."],"others":"Same properties in every direction is isotropy, which is true of amorphous solids. A crystal has one melting point, not different ones in different directions. Crystals do have a regular arrangement.","trap":"Isotropic = same everywhere (amorphous). Anisotropic = depends on direction (crystal).","answer":"some physical properties, such as refractive index, differ in different directions"},"chem-12-01-m03":{"idea":"Match the properties to the type of solid using the force between particles.","steps":["Hard and brittle means strong forces between fixed particles that break when shifted.","No conduction in the solid means no free ions or electrons.","Conduction after melting means ions become free to move.","These properties point to an ionic solid."],"others":"A covalent network solid is hard but does not conduct when molten. A molecular solid is soft with a low melting point. A metallic solid conducts in the solid state.","answer":"an ionic solid"},"chem-12-01-m04":{"idea":"Diamond is a giant structure of carbon atoms joined by strong bonds.","steps":["Each carbon is bonded to four other carbons by covalent bonds.","This bonding runs through the whole crystal, so it is a network.","To melt it, many strong covalent bonds must break, so it is very hard with a very high melting point.","It has no free electrons, so it does not conduct."],"others":"Van der Waals forces are weak, so a molecular solid would be soft. Metallic solids conduct electricity. An ionic solid has ions, but diamond has only carbon atoms.","answer":"a covalent (network) solid"},"chem-12-01-m05":{"idea":"Think about what holds CO₂ molecules together in solid form.","steps":["Solid CO₂ is made of separate CO₂ molecules.","Only weak van der Waals forces hold the molecules together.","So it is soft and sublimes easily.","Such a solid is called a molecular solid."],"others":"Metallic and ionic solids need metal atoms or ions. A covalent network solid like diamond is very hard, but dry ice is soft and sublimes.","answer":"a molecular solid"},"chem-12-01-m06":{"idea":"Metal properties come from the way electrons behave in the lattice.","steps":["Valence electrons in a metal are not tied to one atom.","They form a sea of mobile electrons around the metal cations.","The moving electrons carry current, so metals conduct.","Layers can slide without breaking bonds, so metals are malleable."],"others":"Ionic bonds are directional and rigid, so ionic solids are brittle. Directional covalent bonds would not let layers slide. Weak van der Waals forces cannot explain high conductivity.","answer":"a sea of mobile electrons shared by metal cations"},"chem-12-01-m07":{"idea":"A crystal is made by repeating one small block again and again.","steps":["That smallest repeating block is the unit cell.","Repeating it in three directions without gaps builds the full lattice.","So the unit cell is the smallest repeating unit."],"others":"Empty space between planes is not a unit cell. A single atom at one point is a lattice point, not a cell. The largest group of atoms seen has no definition and is not the repeating unit.","answer":"the smallest repeating unit that builds the whole crystal lattice"},"chem-12-01-m08":{"idea":"Count atoms in a cell by sharing: an atom shared with other cells only counts partly.","steps":["Corner atom: shared by 8 cells, counts 1/8. Face atom: shared by 2 cells, counts 1/2. Body centre: counts 1.","Simple cubic: 8 × 1/8 = 1.","BCC: 8 × 1/8 + 1 = 2.","FCC: 8 × 1/8 + 6 × 1/2 = 4."],"others":"Choices with 4 or 6 for BCC, or with 2 for simple cubic, do not match the counts above. Simple cubic must be 1, BCC must be 2, FCC must be 4.","trap":"Remember 1, 2, 4 for SC, BCC, FCC.","answer":"1, 2 and 4"},"chem-12-01-m09":{"idea":"Coordination number is the number of nearest neighbours that touch an atom.","steps":["In a close-packed layer, an atom touches 6 neighbours.","It also touches 3 atoms in the layer above and 3 in the layer below.","Total = 6 + 3 + 3 = 12.","FCC (cubic close packing) therefore has coordination number 12."],"others":"8 is the value for body-centred cubic. 4 is the value for diamond. 6 is the value for simple cubic.","trap":"Close packing (FCC or HCP) = 12.","answer":"12 (the same as in hexagonal close packing)"},"chem-12-01-m10":{"idea":"Look at the central atom in a body-centred cubic cell.","steps":["The atom at the centre touches the atoms at the 8 corners.","Each atom in BCC has the same surroundings.","So the coordination number is 8."],"others":"6 is for simple cubic. 12 is for close-packed structures. 4 is not the value for any common cubic cell.","answer":"8"},"chem-12-01-m11":{"idea":"Packing efficiency tells what percentage of space is filled by atoms.","steps":["FCC and HCP are close-packed structures.","They are the most efficient.","Atoms fill about 74% of the volume."],"others":"52.4% is simple cubic. 68% is body-centred cubic. 90% is far too high; no packing of equal spheres reaches it.","trap":"SC 52.4%, BCC 68%, FCC 74%.","answer":"74%"},"chem-12-01-m12":{"idea":"Arrange by packing efficiency from lowest to highest.","steps":["Simple cubic: 52.4%.","Body-centred cubic: 68%.","Face-centred cubic: 74%.","Increasing order: simple cubic, BCC, FCC."],"others":"The orders that start with FCC or BCC, or that put FCC before BCC, are wrong because FCC is the most efficient and simple cubic is the least.","trap":"Increasing means lowest first.","answer":"simple cubic, body-centred cubic, face-centred cubic"},"chem-12-01-m13":{"idea":"Close packing stacks layers in a pattern. HCP and CCP differ in the third layer.","steps":["In HCP the third layer lies over the first, so the pattern is ABAB.","In CCP the third layer sits over different gaps, so the pattern is ABCABC.","So hexagonal is ABAB and cubic is ABCABC."],"others":"The choice that swaps hexagonal and cubic is wrong. The two patterns are not the same for both types, so ABAB for both and ABCABC for both cannot be right.","answer":"ABAB... for hexagonal and ABCABC... for cubic"},"chem-12-01-m14":{"idea":"Voids are gaps between close-packed atoms. Count them per atom.","steps":["Each atom in close packing has one octahedral void.","Each atom has two tetrahedral voids.","For N atoms: tetrahedral voids = 2N and octahedral voids = N."],"others":"The other pairs switch the numbers or use equal numbers. Tetrahedral voids are the more numerous ones, twice the number of atoms.","trap":"Tetrahedral 2N, octahedral N.","answer":"2N and N"},"chem-12-01-m15":{"idea":"Compare the two voids by how many atoms surround them.","steps":["A tetrahedral void is surrounded by 4 atoms.","An octahedral void is surrounded by 6 atoms.","More surrounding atoms means a bigger gap.","Octahedral radius is about 0.414R and tetrahedral about 0.225R."],"others":"Tetrahedral is the smaller one. Voids are real spaces and do have size. They do not have the same size.","answer":"the octahedral void"},"chem-12-01-m16":{"idea":"In FCC the atoms touch along the face diagonal, not along the edge.","steps":["Face diagonal = a√2, from the Pythagoras rule.","Along this diagonal there are r + 2r + r = 4r.","So a√2 = 4r.","a = 4r/√2 = 2√2 r."],"others":"a = 4r/√3 belongs to BCC. a = √2 r and a = 2r do not fit the face diagonal. a = 2r is for simple cubic.","trap":"FCC: face diagonal. BCC: body diagonal.","answer":"a = 2√2 r"},"chem-12-01-m17":{"idea":"In BCC the atoms touch along the body diagonal.","steps":["Body diagonal = a√3.","It contains r + 2r + r = 4r.","So a√3 = 4r.","a = 4r/√3."],"others":"a = 4r√3 puts the root on the wrong side. a = 2r is for simple cubic. a = 2√2 r is for FCC.","trap":"Body diagonal is a√3; face diagonal is a√2.","answer":"a = 4r/√3"},"chem-12-01-m18":{"idea":"A Schottky defect is a vacancy defect in ionic crystals.","steps":["Equal numbers of cations and anions leave their sites.","So the crystal stays neutral.","With ions gone, the mass per volume drops, so density falls.","It occurs when cation and anion are similar in size, as in NaCl."],"others":"An electron trapped in a vacancy describes an F-centre. A cation moving to an interstitial site is a Frenkel defect. Only cations missing would make the crystal charged, and density would not rise.","answer":"equal numbers of cations and anions from their lattice sites, which lowers the density"},"chem-12-01-m19":{"idea":"A Frenkel defect is a dislocation defect where a small ion moves.","steps":["The small Ag⁺ ion leaves its place.","It sits in a gap between ions (interstitial).","No ion leaves the crystal, so the mass and volume stay the same.","So the density does not change."],"others":"A missing cation and anion is Schottky and lowers density. Extra cations raising density is not Frenkel, since the ion count is unchanged. An anion in an interstitial site is not the usual case, as anions are large.","answer":"a cation leaves its lattice site and sits in an interstitial position, with no change in density"},"chem-12-01-m20":{"idea":"Yellow colour in NaCl heated in sodium vapour comes from a special defect.","steps":["Sodium atoms enter and give electrons; Cl⁻ vacancies are formed.","The electrons get trapped in these anionic vacancies.","These trapped electrons absorb visible light.","This colour centre is called an F-centre."],"others":"Schottky and Frenkel defects do not give colour. Free sodium atoms between layers is not the explanation; the electrons are trapped in vacancies.","answer":"F-centres, anionic vacancies occupied by trapped electrons"},"chem-12-01-m21":{"idea":"n-type means extra negative carriers, so the dopant needs extra electrons.","steps":["Silicon is group 14 with four valence electrons.","A group 15 atom such as arsenic has five.","Four form bonds and the fifth is free.","Free electrons carry current: n-type."],"others":"Group 13 gives p-type. Argon, group 18, and carbon, group 14, add no extra electrons.","trap":"n = negative = group 15. p = positive hole = group 13.","answer":"an element of group 15, such as arsenic"},"chem-12-01-m22":{"idea":"p-type means holes, so the dopant has fewer electrons than silicon.","steps":["A group 13 atom such as boron has three valence electrons.","One bond is missing an electron, which leaves a hole.","The hole moves like a positive charge: p-type."],"others":"Group 15 (phosphorus) gives n-type. Germanium is group 14 like silicon, so it has no effect. Chlorine in group 17 does not make a hole carrier.","answer":"an element of group 13, such as boron"},"chem-12-01-m23":{"idea":"Band theory explains conduction by the energy gap between two bands.","steps":["Electrons conduct only if they reach the conduction band.","In an insulator, the gap between filled valence band and empty conduction band is large.","Electrons cannot cross it at ordinary temperature."],"others":"Overlap of the bands is a metal. Insulators do have valence electrons. A partly filled valence band is a metal.","answer":"a large energy gap between the filled valence band and the empty conduction band"},"chem-12-01-m24":{"idea":"Look at the effect of unpaired electrons in a magnetic field.","steps":["Unpaired electrons have magnetic moments.","In a field they turn towards it.","The substance is weakly attracted, and loses magnetism when the field is removed.","This is paramagnetism, as in O₂ and Cu²⁺."],"others":"Ferromagnetic substances are strongly attracted and stay magnetised. Antiferromagnetic substances have moments cancelling. Diamagnetic substances are weakly repelled.","answer":"paramagnetic"},"chem-12-01-m25":{"idea":"Strong attraction and permanent magnetism point to one type.","steps":["In iron, cobalt and nickel, magnetic moments line up in the same direction in domains.","This gives strong attraction.","The magnetism can be permanent.","This is ferromagnetism."],"others":"Antiferromagnetic means moments cancel. Diamagnetic is weak repulsion. Paramagnetic is weak attraction that stops when the field is removed.","answer":"ferromagnetic"},"chem-12-01-m26":{"idea":"All electrons paired means no permanent magnetic moment.","steps":["NaCl and water have all electrons paired.","The field induces a small opposite moment.","So they are weakly repelled.","This is diamagnetism."],"others":"Ferromagnetic, ferrimagnetic and paramagnetic substances all need unpaired electrons, so none fits paired electrons.","answer":"diamagnetic"},"chem-12-01-m27":{"idea":"Density is mass divided by volume. Get both for one unit cell.","steps":["Mass of one atom = M/N_A. With Z atoms, mass = ZM/N_A.","Volume of the cubic cell = a³.","ρ = mass/volume = ZM/(a³N_A)."],"others":"ZMa³/N_A multiplies by volume instead of dividing. M/(Za³N_A) puts Z in the wrong place. ZN_A/(Ma³) uses the inverse of the mass term.","trap":"Check units: g/mol × atoms ÷ (cm³ × atoms/mol) = g/cm³.","answer":"ZM/(a³ N_A)"},"chem-12-01-n01":{"idea":"In FCC the atoms touch along the face diagonal. Link a and r.","steps":["Face diagonal = a√2 = 4r.","r = a/(2√2).","r = 361/(2 × 1.414) = 361/2.828.","r = 127.6 pm."],"trap":"Check: r must be less than a/2 = 180.5 pm, and it is.","answer":"127.6 pm"},"chem-12-01-n02":{"idea":"In BCC the atoms touch along the body diagonal.","steps":["Body diagonal = a√3 = 4r.","a = 4r/√3.","a = 4 × 124/1.732 = 496/1.732.","a = 286.4 pm."],"trap":"Check: a is a bit more than 2r = 248 pm, and it is.","answer":"286.4 pm"},"chem-12-01-n03":{"idea":"Use the density formula for a cell: ρ = ZM/(a³N_A). FCC has Z = 4.","steps":["a = 361 pm = 3.61 × 10⁻⁸ cm, so a³ = 4.704 × 10⁻²³ cm³.","Mass of cell = 4 × 63.5 / 6.022 × 10²³ = 4.218 × 10⁻²² g.","ρ = 4.218 × 10⁻²² / 4.704 × 10⁻²³.","ρ = 8.97 g/cm³."],"trap":"Convert pm to cm first: 1 pm = 10⁻¹⁰ cm.","answer":"8.97 g/cm³"},"chem-12-01-n04":{"idea":"Packing efficiency = volume of atoms in the cell ÷ volume of the cell.","steps":["FCC has 4 atoms, so atom volume = 4 × (4/3)πr³ = (16/3)πr³.","a = 2√2 r, so a³ = 16√2 r³.","Efficiency = (16π/3) / (16√2) = π/(3√2).","= 0.7405, which is 74.05%."],"trap":"Answer must be below 100%. Known value for close packing is about 74%.","answer":"74.05 %"},"chem-12-01-n05":{"idea":"Find the number of atoms first, then divide by 4 atoms per FCC cell.","steps":["63.5 g of Cu is 1 mol, which is 6.022 × 10²³ atoms.","Atoms per FCC cell = 4.","Cells = 6.022 × 10²³ / 4.","= 1.5055 × 10²³, about 1.51 × 10²³."],"trap":"Answer is given in units of 10²³, so write 1.51.","answer":"1.51 × 10²³"},"chem-12-01-n06":{"idea":"Rearrange ρ = ZM/(a³N_A) to find a³.","steps":["a³ = ZM/(ρN_A) = 4 × 58.5 / (2.165 × 6.022 × 10²³).","= 234 / 1.3038 × 10²⁴ = 1.795 × 10⁻²² cm³.","a = cube root = 5.64 × 10⁻⁸ cm.","1 pm = 10⁻¹⁰ cm, so a = 564 pm."],"trap":"Check: the NaCl edge is about 5.6 Å, which is 564 pm.","answer":"564 pm"},"chem-12-01-n07":{"idea":"In close packing each atom has two tetrahedral voids.","steps":["Number of atoms = 2.0 × 10²².","Tetrahedral voids = 2 × number of atoms.","= 2 × 2.0 × 10²² = 4.0 × 10²².","In units of 10²² this is 4.0."],"trap":"Octahedral voids would be equal to the atoms, 2.0 × 10²².","answer":"4.0 × 10²²"},"chem-12-01-n08":{"idea":"In simple cubic, atoms touch along the edge, so a = 2r.","steps":["a = 2r = 2 × 100 = 200 pm.","Convert: 200 pm = 200 × 10⁻¹⁰ cm = 2 × 10⁻⁸ cm.","V = a³ = (2 × 10⁻⁸)³ = 8 × 10⁻²⁴ cm³."],"trap":"Cube the number and its power: (10⁻⁸)³ = 10⁻²⁴.","answer":"8.0 × 10⁻²⁴ cm³"},"chem-12-01-n09":{"idea":"A unit cell mass is the number of atoms in it times the mass of one atom.","steps":["FCC has 4 atoms per cell.","Mass of one Cu atom = 63.5 / 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.0544 × 10⁻²² g.","Mass of cell = 4 × 1.0544 × 10⁻²² g.","= 4.22 × 10⁻²² g."],"trap":"Same value as the numerator ZM/N_A in the density formula.","answer":"4.22 × 10⁻²² g"},"chem-12-01-n10":{"idea":"A BCC cell has Z = 2. Use the density formula to find a³.","steps":["a³ = ZM/(ρN_A) = 2 × 50 / (5.96 × 6.022 × 10²³).","= 100 / 3.589 × 10²⁴ = 2.786 × 10⁻²³ cm³.","a = cube root = 3.03 × 10⁻⁸ cm.","a = 303.2 pm."],"trap":"Cube roots are easier if you write the volume as a number times 10⁻²⁴ first.","answer":"303.2 pm"}};
